          content: `Step 1: Create a Python file
- Open VS Code
- Create a new file: File > New File
- Save it as hello.py (the .py extension is crucial)

Step 2: Write your first program
print("Hello, World!")

Step 3: Run your program
In terminal: python hello.py
You should see: Hello, World!

What print() does:
- print() is a built-in function that outputs text to the console
- It can print multiple things separated by commas
- It adds a newline at the end by default

How Python executes:
Python reads your file from top to bottom, line by line. Each line is executed in order. This is sequential execution — unlike some languages that might analyze the whole program first.

Comments:
Use # to add comments that Python ignores. Comments explain your code to other developers (and your future self).

# This is a comment
print("Hello")  # This is also a comment

Python REPL:
You can also run Python interactively. Type python in your terminal to enter the REPL (Read-Eval-Print Loop). Type code and see results immediately. Press Ctrl+D or exit() to quit.`,
