        {
          id: "ch1-how-python-runs",
          title: "How Python Code Runs",
          whyItMatters: "Understanding Python's execution model helps you write better code and debug issues. It explains why Python behaves differently from compiled languages.",
          realWorldAnalogy: "Python is like an interpreter at the United Nations — it translates your code to machine language on the fly, line by line, rather than translating everything beforehand like a compiled language.",
          content: `Python is an interpreted language, not a compiled language. Here's the execution flow:

1. Source Code (.py file)
   The Python code you write in plain text

2. Python Interpreter (CPython)
   Reads and translates your code to bytecode

3. Bytecode (.pyc file)
   Intermediate representation that's faster to execute
   Python caches this in __pycache__ folder

4. Python Virtual Machine (PVM)
   Executes the bytecode line by line

5. CPU
   Actually runs the instructions

CPython vs Other Implementations:
- CPython: The default implementation (written in C)
- PyPy: Just-in-time compiler (faster for long-running programs)
- Jython: Python running on Java Virtual Machine
- IronPython: Python running on .NET

Why Python is Slower than C:
C compiles directly to machine code before running. Python interprets bytecode at runtime. The interpretation overhead makes Python slower, but for most applications, this doesn't matter. Developer productivity is more important than raw execution speed.

The Global Interpreter Lock (GIL):
CPython has a GIL — only one thread can execute Python bytecode at a time. This means Python can't truly use multiple CPU cores for CPU-bound tasks. However, for I/O-bound tasks (networking, file operations), this isn't a problem. We'll cover threading and multiprocessing in later chapters.`,
          codeExamples: [
