          whyItMatters: "Python has 8 built-in data types. Understanding each type and when to use it is fundamental to writing correct Python programs.",
          realWorldAnalogy: "Data types are like different containers — you wouldn't put soup in a paper bag or water in a cardboard box. Each data type is designed for specific kinds of data.",
          content: `TYPE 1: int — Integers (whole numbers)
age = 25
score = -10
count = 0
billion = 1_000_000_000  # underscores for readability

TYPE 2: float — Decimals (floating-point numbers)
price = 19.99
temperature = -3.5
pi = 3.14159
scientific = 1.5e-10  # scientific notation

TYPE 3: str — Strings (text)
name = "Alice"
city = 'Dhaka'
message = \"\"\"Multi-line
string\"\"\"

TYPE 4: bool — Boolean (True or False)
is_active = True
is_complete = False
Note: Capital T and F (unlike JavaScript's true/false)

TYPE 5: list — Ordered, mutable collection
fruits = ["apple", "banana", "mango"]
numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
mixed = [1, "hello", 3.14, True]

TYPE 6: tuple — Ordered, IMMUTABLE collection
coords = (23.7, 90.4)
rgb = (255, 128, 0)
single = (42,)  # Note trailing comma for single-element tuple

TYPE 7: dict — Key-value pairs
person = {"name": "Alice", "age": 25}
settings = {"theme": "dark", "notifications": True}

TYPE 8: set — Unordered unique values
unique = {1, 2, 3, 3, 2}  # Becomes {1, 2, 3}
letters = {'a', 'b', 'c'}

Plus: None — Python's equivalent of null/undefined
result = None
not_found = None`,
          codeExamples: [
            {
              id: "ch2-all-types",
              title: "All 8 Data Types",
              description: "Examples of each data type",
              code: { python: "# Integer\nage = 25\nprint(f\"int: {age}, type: {type(age)}\")\n\n# Float\nprice = 19.99\nprint(f\"float: {price}, type: {type(price)}\")\n\n# String\nname = \"Alice\"\nprint(f\"str: {name}, type: {type(name)}\")\n\n# Boolean\nis_active = True\nprint(f\"bool: {is_active}, type: {type(is_active)}\")\n\n# List\nfruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\"]\nprint(f\"list: {fruits}, type: {type(fruits)}\")\n\n# Tuple\ncoords = (23.7, 90.4)\nprint(f\"tuple: {coords}, type: {type(coords)}\")\n\n# Dictionary\nperson = {\"name\": \"Alice\"}\nprint(f\"dict: {person}, type: {type(person)}\")\n\n# Set\nunique = {1, 2, 3}\nprint(f\"set: {unique}, type: {type(unique)}\")\n\n# None\nnothing = None\nprint(f\"None: {nothing}, type: {type(nothing)}\")" },
              explanation: "Each data type serves a specific purpose. Use type() to check a value's type."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: "ch2-type-checking",
          title: "The type() Function and isinstance()",
          whyItMatters: "Type checking helps you debug issues and ensure your code works with the right kinds of data. isinstance() is especially useful for control flow and validation.",
          realWorldAnalogy: "Type checking is like checking the label on a food container before cooking — you want to make sure you have the right ingredient before you proceed.",
          content: `The type() function returns the type of a value:

type(42)        → <class 'int'>
type("hello")   → <class 'str'>
type(True)      → <class 'bool'>
type([1, 2, 3]) → <class 'list'>

The isinstance() function checks if a value is of a specific type:

isinstance(42, int)        → True
isinstance("hello", str)   → True
isinstance(42, str)        → False
isinstance(42, (int, float)) → True  # Check multiple types

Why isinstance() is preferred over type():
isinstance() works with inheritance (important for OOP)
isinstance() can check multiple types at once
isinstance() is more Pythonic

Type Checking in Practice:
Use type checking when:
- Validating user input
- Debugging type errors
- Writing flexible functions that accept multiple types
- Implementing type-specific behavior

Python is dynamically typed but strongly typed:
This means:
- You don't declare types (dynamic)
- Types don't change implicitly (strong)
- 1 + "2" throws TypeError (unlike JavaScript which gives "12")

This prevents many bugs that occur in loosely-typed languages.`,
          codeExamples: [
            {
              id: "ch2-type-check",
              title: "Type Checking",
              description: "Using type() and isinstance()",
              code: { python: "# Using type()\nvalue = 42\nprint(f\"Type of {value}: {type(value)}\")\n\nvalue = \"hello\"\nprint(f\"Type of {value}: {type(value)}\")\n\n# Using isinstance()\nprint(f\"42 is int: {isinstance(42, int)}\")\nprint(f\"'hello' is str: {isinstance('hello', str)}\")\nprint(f\"42 is str: {isinstance(42, str)}\")\n\n# Check multiple types\nprint(f\"42 is int or float: {isinstance(42, (int, float))}\")\nprint(f\"'hello' is int or float: {isinstance('hello', (int, float))}\")" },
              explanation: "type() returns the type, isinstance() checks if a value matches a type. isinstance() is more flexible and Pythonic."
            },
            {
              id: "ch2-strong-typing",
              title: "Strong Typing",
              description: "Python prevents implicit type conversion",
              code: { python: "# Python is strongly typed - no implicit conversion\ntry:\n    result = 1 + \"2\"  # Error!\n    print(result)\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n\n# Explicit conversion required\nresult = str(1) + \"2\"\nprint(f\"String concatenation: {result}\")\n\nresult = 1 + int(\"2\")\nprint(f\"Numeric addition: {result}\")" },
              explanation: "Python requires explicit type conversion, preventing bugs from implicit type coercion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: "ch2-type-conversion",
          title: "Type Conversion (Casting)",
          whyItMatters: "Type conversion lets you transform data between types, which is essential for user input, file operations, and data processing.",
          realWorldAnalogy: "Type conversion is like translating between languages — the meaning stays the same but the representation changes to fit the context.",
          content: `Converting Between Types:

int("42")        → 42      # String to integer
int(3.14)        → 3       # Float to integer (truncates)
int(True)        → 1       # Boolean to integer

str(42)          → "42"    # Integer to string
str(3.14)        → "3.14"  # Float to string
str(True)        → "True"  # Boolean to string

float("3.14")    → 3.14    # String to float
float(42)        → 42.0    # Integer to float

bool(0)          → False   # Zero to False
bool(1)          → True    # Non-zero to True
bool("")         → False   # Empty string to False
bool("hello")    → True    # Non-empty string to True

Truthy and Falsy Values in Python:
Falsy values (evaluate to False in boolean context):
- 0, 0.0 (zero numbers)
- "" (empty string)
- [], (), {} (empty collections)
- None
- False

Everything else is truthy (evaluates to True):
- Non-zero numbers
- Non-empty strings
- Non-empty collections
- True

Common Use Cases:
- Converting user input (always string) to numbers
- Formatting numbers as strings for display
- Converting boolean values to integers for calculations`,
          codeExamples: [
            {
              id: "ch2-conversion",
              title: "Type Conversion Examples",
              description: "Converting between different types",
              code: { python: "# String to number\nage_str = \"25\"\nage = int(age_str)\nprint(f\"Age as number: {age}\")\n\n# Number to string\nscore = 100\nscore_str = str(score)\nprint(f\"Score as string: '{score_str}'\")\n\n# Float to int (truncates)\nprice = 19.99\nprice_int = int(price)\nprint(f\"Price as int: {price_int}\")\n\n# Boolean conversion\nprint(f\"bool(0): {bool(0)}\")\nprint(f\"bool(1): {bool(1)}\")\nprint(f\"bool(''): {bool('')}\")\nprint(f\"bool('hello'): {bool('hello')}\")" },
              explanation: "Use int(), str(), float(), and bool() to convert between types. Note that float to int truncates (doesn't round)."
            },
            {
              id: "ch2-truthy-falsy",
              title: "Truthy and Falsy Values",
              description: "Understanding boolean evaluation",
              code: { python: "# Falsy values\nprint(f\"bool(0): {bool(0)}\")\nprint(f\"bool(0.0): {bool(0.0)}\")\nprint(f\"bool(''): {bool('')}\")\nprint(f\"bool([]): {bool([])}\")\nprint(f"bool({{}}): {bool({})}\")\nprint(f\"bool(None): {bool(None)}\")\n\n# Truthy values\nprint(f\"bool(1): {bool(1)}\")\nprint(f\"bool(-1): {bool(-1)}\")\nprint(f\"bool('hello'): {bool('hello')}\")\nprint(f\"bool([1]): {bool([1])}\")\n\n# Using in conditions\nvalue = 0\nif value:\n    print(\"Value is truthy\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Value is falsy\")" },
              explanation: "In Python, values can be used directly in boolean contexts. Falsy values evaluate to False, everything else to True."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          id: "ch2-multiple-assignment",
          title: "Multiple Assignment and Swap",
          whyItMatters: "Python's multiple assignment features make code more concise and readable. The swap trick is particularly elegant and eliminates the need for temporary variables.",
          realWorldAnalogy: "Multiple assignment is like handing out multiple gifts at once instead of one at a time — more efficient and cleaner.",
          content: `Multiple Assignment:

Assign the same value to multiple variables:
x = y = z = 0
All three variables now point to the value 0

Assign different values in one line (tuple unpacking):
a, b, c = 1, 2, 3
a gets 1, b gets 2, c gets 3

The Python Swap Trick:
Swap two variables without a temporary variable:
a, b = b, a
This is Python magic — it creates a tuple (b, a), then unpacks it to a, b

Augmented Assignment:
Instead of x = x + 1, use:
x += 1    # x = x + 1
x -= 1    # x = x - 1
x *= 2    # x = x * 2
x /= 2    # x = x / 2
x //= 3   # x = x // 3 (floor division)
x %= 5    # x = x % 5 (modulo)
x **= 2   # x = x ** 2 (power)

Augmented assignment is:
- More concise
- More readable
- Potentially faster (some optimizations)

Unpacking Lists:
You can unpack lists into variables:
fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]
first, second, third = fruits

Extended Unpacking (Python 3.5+):
first, *rest = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
first gets 1, rest gets [2, 3, 4, 5]`,
          codeExamples: [
            {
              id: "ch2-multi-assign",
              title: "Multiple Assignment",
              description: "Various assignment techniques",
              code: { python: "# Same value to multiple variables\nx = y = z = 0\nprint(f\"x={x}, y={y}, z={z}\")\n\n# Different values (tuple unpacking)\na, b, c = 1, 2, 3\nprint(f\"a={a}, b={b}, c={c}\")\n\n# The Python swap trick\nx, y = 5, 10\nprint(f\"Before swap: x={x}, y={y}\")\nx, y = y, x\nprint(f\"After swap: x={x}, y={y}\")\n\n# Augmented assignment\nnum = 10\nnum += 5\nprint(f\"num += 5: {num}\")\nnum *= 2\nprint(f\"num *= 2: {num}\")" },
              explanation: "Python's assignment features make code concise. The swap trick (a, b = b, a) is particularly elegant."
            },
            {
              id: "ch2-unpacking",
              title: "List Unpacking",
              description: "Unpacking lists and extended unpacking",
              code: { python: "# Basic list unpacking\nfruits = [\"apple\", \"banana\", \"cherry\"]\nfirst, second, third = fruits\nprint(f\"{first}, {second}, {third}\")\n\n# Extended unpacking\nnumbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nfirst, *rest = numbers\nprint(f\"First: {first}\")\nprint(f\"Rest: {rest}\")\n\n# First and last\nfirst, *middle, last = numbers\nprint(f\"First: {first}, Middle: {middle}, Last: {last}\")" },
              explanation: "Unpacking lets you assign list elements to variables. Extended unpacking with * captures remaining elements."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
